  "body": "The maintainer reported this in chat on 2026-11-16. His words: \"there is flags on camcraft-ui app, but \"--help\" wont show the options for mcp and it settings for example or overriding gpu and such\"\n\n## What happens\n\nThe `camcraft-ui` program reads start options, but `--help` does not show them. The user cannot find them without the source.\n\nThe options that the source reads (`crates/ui/src/egui_app/mod.rs`, `crates/ui/src/egui_app/gpu.rs`, `crates/ui/src/lib.rs`):\n\n- `--gpu-backend <name>` and the variable `CAMCRAFT_GPU_BACKEND`\n- `--gpu <adapter name>` and the variable `CAMCRAFT_GPU`\n- the MCP port option and the variable `CAMCRAFT_MCP_PORT`\n- `--headless`\n- `--ui`\n\nThe worker must find each option and each variable that the program reads. This list can be incomplete.\n\n## Expected\n\n`camcraft-ui --help` prints each start option and each environment variable, with its value form, its default and one line that says what it does. It then exits with code 0 and opens no window.\n\n## What to do\n\n1. Keep the options in one table (name, value form, environment variable, default, help line). The parser and the help text read the same table, so that they cannot disagree.\n2. `--help` and `-h` print the help text and exit before a window or a GPU device is made.\n3. `--version` prints the version and exits.\n4. An option that the program does not know gives a clean error that names it and points to `--help`. The program does not start with a wrong option ignored.\n5. Write the help text in plain English (no G-code terms).\n6. Add the options to the user guide page that describes how to start the program.\n\n## Done means\n\n- A test asserts that the help text names each option of the table and each environment variable.\n- A test asserts that each flag string that the parser reads is in the table.\n- A test asserts that an unknown option gives the error and no window.\n- The PR body has the printed `--help` text.\n",
